<Project>

  <!--
    The repository-wide TARGET FRAMEWORK, and the one line a .NET hop edits.

    Every csproj family in this tree - the converter-emitted standard library and behavioral
    tests, the emitted Phase-4 test hosts, the hand-written runtime and tooling projects - used
    to carry its own hardcoded <TargetFramework>. A framework hop therefore meant rewriting the
    two converter templates, every hand-written project, and then regenerating the whole corpus
    to level the emitted copies. Hoisting the value here makes the hop a single edit.

    Why the projects keep a CONDITIONED fallback rather than dropping the property outright:
    an emitted project does not always have this file above it. deploy-core.ps1 stages the
    corpus under %GOPATH%\src\go2cs and writes its OWN root props (which pins $(go2csPath) and
    deliberately excludes core's), a -recurse conversion writes generated code under an
    arbitrary output root, and a single-package conversion can land anywhere at all. In those
    trees nothing would define the property and the build would fail outright. Directory.Build
    .props is imported ABOVE the project body, so where this file IS in scope it wins and the
    project's own line is inert; where it is not, the project still names a framework and still
    builds. The hop is one line here, and the emitted copies level on the next regeneration.

    Discovery note: MSBuild stops at the FIRST Directory.Build.props found walking up, so the
    two nested ones (src/core, src/tests/Performance) explicitly import this file. Any new
    nested props file owes the same import or it silently shadows this one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework Condition="'$(TargetFramework)'==''">net10.0</TargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The repository-wide LANGUAGE VERSION, the second line a .NET hop edits, hoisted the same way and
    for the same reason as the framework above (each emitted project keeps only a CONDITIONED
    fallback of the same value, for trees this file is not above).

    Pinned, not `latest`: `latest` follows whichever SDK builds the tree, so an 11.x SDK would silently
    move the whole corpus to C# 15 and its new contextual keywords (C# 14's `field` already broke the
    source generator once, issue #34). 14 is the net10.0 default. Its companion is /global.json, which
    pins the SDK band (10.0.100 floor, `latestFeature`) so an 11.x SDK is refused by name rather than
    trusted. Never use a `latestMajor` roll-forward there: it is the only policy that crosses a major
    and would admit 11.x. The source generator (src/gen/go2cs-gen, netstandard2.0) keeps its own
    explicit, unconditional 14 in its csproj: a netstandard2.0 default is C# 7.3, so it must never be
    left to a default or to this file being above it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion Condition="'$(LangVersion)'==''">14</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    A source generator that fails to LOAD or INITIALIZE is only a warning to the compiler: CS8034 (the
    analyzer assembly cannot be loaded), CS8032 (it cannot be instantiated), CS8784 (a generator failed to
    initialize), CS8785 (a generator threw). The build goes on WITHOUT the generator's output and exits 0, or
    fails later as a CS0246 on the first generated type the code names, far from the cause and passing on a
    rerun. Raised to errors, a missing go2cs-gen names itself. APPENDED, so nothing set earlier is dropped.
    Census before this line: 0 of the four codes across 150 compilations of reflect's test closure (C2,
    2026-09-30). Guard: repoguard's TestGeneratorLoadFailuresAreBuildErrors.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS8032;CS8034;CS8784;CS8785</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
